:: Start
<div class="eyebrow">AP Language · A fictional author encounter</div><h1>The Ride That Wouldn’t Autocorrect</h1><p>A hidden WeChat mini-program. One magazine deadline. More than one way to be heard.</p><p>10 decisions · 12–15 minutes plus writing · No timer · Unlimited replays</p><p>The classroom encounter, dialogue, people and outcomes are invented. Only labelled excerpts are Young’s actual words. This story complements the assigned reading.</p><<if window.parent === window>><<button "Begin standalone preview">><<run setup.ready=true;setup.refresh()>><</button>><<else>><p>Connecting to your class…</p><</if>>

:: Decision1
<div class="novel"><aside class="scene-side"><<ownStage 0>><<ownStats>><div class="charge">Decision 1 of 10</div></aside><section class="dialogue"><div class="eyebrow">The Ride That Wouldn’t Autocorrect</div><h1>The ride that wouldn’t autocorrect</h1><p class="speaker">Lin · student writer</p><div class="story-beats"><p>Mr. Hepting opens WeChat, taps a mini-program hidden behind an extremely ordinary dumpling icon, and orders a DiDi. “Field trip. Bring an objection.” Outside, a small car unfolds into a classroom-sized cabin. The driver declines to explain the geometry.</p><p>Lin shows you a message from the school magazine: “Your argument about belonging is strong. We can publish it Friday if you remove the home-language expressions. Some readers may not understand them.” Mei has a deadline and a real concern about readers. Lin wants the byline—but wants to recognize the voice beneath it.</p><p>Lin has asked you to be a writing partner. You can advise; the final submission remains Lin’s decision. The car’s destination changes to a lecture by Vershawn Ashanti Young. Before you arrive, what do you promise?</p></div><div class="after-beats"><div class="choice-heading">Your next move</div><<ownChoice "reach" "Help Lin reach the magazine’s readers, while checking each proposed edit.">>
<<ownChoice "voice" "Help Lin preserve the voice, while looking for ways to orient unfamiliar readers.">>
<<ownChoice "decide" "Promise to persuade Lin to reject every change before seeing the edits.">></div></section></div>

:: Consequence1
<div class="novel"><aside class="scene-side"><<ownStage 0 true>><<ownStats>></aside><section class="dialogue"><h1>The ride that wouldn’t autocorrect</h1><div class="story-beats"><<ownConsequence 0>></div><div class="after-beats"><<button "Continue">><<goto "Decision2">><</button>></div></section></div>

:: Decision2
<div class="novel"><aside class="scene-side"><<ownStage 1>><<ownStats>><div class="charge">Decision 2 of 10</div></aside><section class="dialogue"><div class="eyebrow">The Ride That Wouldn’t Autocorrect</div><h1>The seat beside you</h1><p class="speaker">Jordan · visiting student</p><div class="story-beats"><p>A visiting student moves a bag so you can sit down. “I’m Jordan. I been working on our zine all week. Folks keep fixing the voice and leaving the weak evidence. That part needs work too.”</p><p>Jordan points to an unsupported claim in a draft. You understand the concern about evidence, but “zine” is unfamiliar. Lin looks from Jordan’s marked-up page to the magazine message.</p></div><div class="after-beats"><div class="choice-heading">Your next move</div><<ownChoice "clarify" "“What kind of publication is your zine? And what evidence would strengthen this claim?”">>
<<ownChoice "engage" "“You’re separating voice from support. Show me the claim you think needs evidence.”">>
<<ownChoice "correct" "“You mean ‘I have been.’ You’ll sound more credible if you fix that first.”">></div></section></div>

:: Consequence2
<div class="novel"><aside class="scene-side"><<ownStage 1 true>><<ownStats>></aside><section class="dialogue"><h1>The seat beside you</h1><div class="story-beats"><<ownConsequence 1>></div><div class="after-beats"><<button "Continue">><<goto "Decision3">><</button>></div></section></div>

:: Decision3
<div class="novel"><aside class="scene-side"><<ownStage 2>><<ownStats>><div class="charge">Decision 3 of 10</div></aside><section class="dialogue"><div class="eyebrow">The Ride That Wouldn’t Autocorrect</div><h1>What the room hears</h1><p class="speaker">Young · fictionalized encounter</p><div class="story-beats"><p>Young places a short quotation from the essay on the screen. Read source card A below. In our imagined lecture, he asks: “Where does this sentence put the cause of harm? What changes when we move the cause?” The printed excerpt is the evidence; the encounter around it is fiction.</p><p>Jordan offers a question: “If I’m responsible for helping someone follow my argument, are they responsible for trying to follow it?” At the back, Mei has joined the class. She says, “I still need to know what an unfamiliar reader actually understands.”</p><p>You can suggest one small investigation before the magazine deadline. What would tell you something useful?</p></div><div class="after-beats"><<ownSource "A">><div class="choice-heading">Your next move</div><<ownChoice "test" "Ask two unfamiliar readers to paraphrase Lin’s claim and identify exactly where they lose the meaning.">>
<<ownChoice "compare" "Compare the original and an edited version: what meaning, emphasis or relationship changes?">>
<<ownChoice "vote" "Ask which version sounds more intelligent, without asking readers what they understood.">></div></section></div>

