:: Decision6
<div class="novel"><aside class="scene-side"><<ownStage 5>><<ownStats>><div class="charge">Decision 6 of 10</div></aside><section class="dialogue"><div class="eyebrow">The Ride That Wouldn’t Autocorrect</div><h1>Question round 3 · transfer</h1><p class="speaker">Jordan · visiting student</p><div class="story-beats"><p>Young directs everyone back to the essay’s sequence of objection and response. Card C is a reading guide, not a replacement for the passage. Open the article if you need the surrounding argument. Now choose where to take the discussion.</p></div><div class="after-beats"><<ownSource "C">><div class="choice-heading">Your next move</div><<ownChoice "china" "How could we explore this in our multilingual school in China?">>
<<ownChoice "revise" "What would a useful magazine revision actually change?">>
<<ownChoice "assess" "What should fair assessment reward?">></div></section></div>

:: Consequence6
<div class="novel"><aside class="scene-side"><<ownStage 5 true>><<ownStats>></aside><section class="dialogue"><h1>Question round 3 · transfer</h1><div class="story-beats"><<ownConsequence 5>></div><div class="after-beats"><<ownQuestionSource 5>><<button "Continue">><<goto "Decision7">><</button>></div></section></div>

:: Decision7
<div class="novel"><aside class="scene-side"><<ownStage 6>><<ownStats>><div class="charge">Decision 7 of 10</div></aside><section class="dialogue"><div class="eyebrow">The Ride That Wouldn’t Autocorrect</div><h1>A reply that goes somewhere</h1><p class="speaker">Mei · magazine editor</p><div class="story-beats"><p>After the lecture, the class gathers in a seminar room. Mei returns to the practical objection: “Our readers need to understand each other. One shared standard seems the fairest way to achieve that.”</p><p>Lin asks you to draft the first move in a response. It must do more than disagree: it needs to move the proposal forward.</p></div><div class="after-beats"><div class="choice-heading">Your next move</div><<ownChoice "bridge" "“Shared understanding matters. Let’s test the confusing passages and add context where needed, rather than assuming every distinctive expression blocks understanding.”">>
<<ownChoice "qualify" "“I want this readership, so I’ll discuss selected changes with Lin. But accepting an edit here wouldn’t prove the original voice is inferior.”">>
<<ownChoice "dismiss" "“Anyone who needs a shared standard is prejudiced, so the magazine should publish the original immediately.”">></div></section></div>

:: Consequence7
<div class="novel"><aside class="scene-side"><<ownStage 6 true>><<ownStats>></aside><section class="dialogue"><h1>A reply that goes somewhere</h1><div class="story-beats"><<ownConsequence 6>></div><div class="after-beats"><<button "Continue">><<goto "Decision8">><</button>></div></section></div>

:: Decision8
<div class="novel"><aside class="scene-side"><<ownStage 7>><<ownStats>><div class="charge">Decision 8 of 10</div></aside><section class="dialogue"><div class="eyebrow">The Ride That Wouldn’t Autocorrect</div><h1>The computer over there</h1><p class="speaker">Young · fictionalized encounter</p><div class="story-beats"><p>In this fictional encounter, Young checks the time. “I have to go to the bathroom, but don’t worry: I’ve uploaded a facsimile of my consciousness into that computer over there. Feel free to ask it a question.”</p><p>The computer lights up: DEFINITELY NOT A CONSCIOUSNESS. Underneath: “AI reading companion. I interpret a small, identified source bundle. I am not Young, cannot speak for him, and may get things wrong.” Lin laughs. “At least it knows what an objection is.”</p><p>You may write one authentic question using the computer panel below. It is optional and never changes your score. Then decide what you will do with an answer.</p></div><div class="after-beats"><<ownComputer>><div class="choice-heading">Your next move</div><<ownChoice "check" "Check the answer against a source card and separate evidence from an inference.">>
<<ownChoice "follow" "Use the answer to form a sharper question for the article, even if it does not settle my view.">>
<<ownChoice "authority" "Treat the answer as Young’s final view because the computer sounds confident.">></div></section></div>

:: Consequence8
<div class="novel"><aside class="scene-side"><<ownStage 7 true>><<ownStats>></aside><section class="dialogue"><h1>The computer over there</h1><div class="story-beats"><<ownConsequence 7>></div><div class="after-beats"><<button "Continue">><<goto "Decision9">><</button>></div></section></div>

:: Decision9
<div class="novel"><aside class="scene-side"><<ownStage 8>><<ownStats>><div class="charge">Decision 9 of 10</div></aside><section class="dialogue"><div class="eyebrow">The Ride That Wouldn’t Autocorrect</div><h1>Friday’s issue</h1><p class="speaker">Lin · student writer</p><div class="story-beats"><p>Young returns. In the imagined encounter he says, “I know I’ve talked a lot. I want to hear what you think now.” Mei has to close the issue soon. Lin is willing to follow any of the three plans below if you can explain it honestly.</p><p>No option guarantees all the things Lin wants. The publication decision determines the ending, not your learning score. What do you recommend?</p></div><div class="after-beats"><div class="choice-heading">Your next move</div><<ownChoice "preserve" "Keep the distinctive language and decline blanket replacement, accepting that this issue may go ahead without the piece.">>
<<ownChoice "negotiate" "Offer contextual clues and selected edits, with Lin approving every change.">>
<<ownChoice "adapt" "Adapt this piece for the magazine’s preferred register, while retaining the original for another use.">></div></section></div>

:: Consequence9
<div class="novel"><aside class="scene-side"><<ownStage 8 true>><<ownStats>></aside><section class="dialogue"><h1>Friday’s issue</h1><div class="story-beats"><<ownConsequence 8>></div><div class="after-beats"><<button "Continue">><<goto "Decision10">><</button>></div></section></div>

:: Decision10
<div class="novel"><aside class="scene-side"><<ownStage 9>><<ownStats>><div class="charge">Decision 10 of 10</div></aside><section class="dialogue"><div class="eyebrow">The Ride That Wouldn’t Autocorrect</div><h1>The ride home</h1><p class="speaker">Lin · student writer</p><div class="story-beats"><p>Back in the classroom, Mr. Hepting’s WeChat receipt says “Distance: one assumption.” The fare is inexplicably a dumpling. Lin opens the original draft beside the latest version.</p><p>Before you see what happens later, return to your first promise. What belongs in the note you leave Lin?</p></div><div class="after-beats"><div class="choice-heading">Your next move</div><<ownChoice "revisit" "Name what changed my advice, what I still value, and one cost the decision does not solve.">>
<<ownChoice "defend" "Keep my initial priority, but explain how evidence changed the way I would carry it out.">>
<<ownChoice "winning" "Say the best decision is whichever gets the most applause.">></div></section></div>

:: Consequence10
<div class="novel"><aside class="scene-side"><<ownStage 9 true>><<ownStats>></aside><section class="dialogue"><h1>The ride home</h1><div class="story-beats"><<ownConsequence 9>></div><div class="after-beats"><<button "Continue">><<goto "Assessment">><</button>></div></section></div>

:: Assessment
<div class="eyebrow">An opportunity is not the end of a story</div><h1>What your choices leave open</h1><<ownAssessment>><details><summary>Recall all source cards</summary><<ownSource>></details><details><summary>Questions you did not choose</summary><h3>What does code-meshing ask a writer to try?</h3><p>“Put the sentence on the desk,” the imagined Young replies. “Try keeping the home-language expression while giving unfamiliar readers a way into its meaning. What does that let you do?” See card B for the published proposal.</p><h3>Does this mean conventions no longer matter?</h3><p>“Show me the edit,” the imagined Young replies. “What does it help a reader understand? What does it change about the voice? Give those questions separate answers.” Read card D before treating code-meshing as the end of revision.</p><h3>What work could a reader do?</h3><p>“What did you do when you met Jordan?” the imagined Young asks. “You could infer, ask, look something up—or make a judgement before listening. Which action gives you evidence?” Return to card A.</p><h3>What if the reader genuinely cannot understand a phrase?</h3><p>“Then we have work to do,” the imagined Young replies. “Try context, an explanation, or a different example. Ask the reader again. Did the change solve the confusion you actually found?”</p><h3>What if the evaluator still insists on a single standard?</h3><p>“Whose risk are you choosing?” the imagined Young asks. “Lin has to live with Friday. Ask what that opportunity means before deciding what someone else should give up. Then explain what your advice does—and cannot do.”</p><h3>Can I question the argument without dismissing it?</h3><p>“Give the disagreement something to stand on,” the imagined Young replies. “State the limit, support it, then tell me what follows. I want an argument I can answer—not a smaller version of my claim that is easier to knock down.”</p><h3>How could we explore this in our multilingual school in China?</h3><p>“Start with examples people choose to share,” the imagined Young suggests. “Who is speaking to whom, for what purpose? Then ask where the comparison helps and where it breaks.” This application does not equate multilingual school experiences with the essay’s racial history.</p><h3>What would a useful magazine revision actually change?</h3><p>The imagined Young folds a page down the middle. “Here: what the edit helps readers understand. There: what it changes about the voice. Give Lin both columns, not just your conclusion.”</p><h3>What should fair assessment reward?</h3><p>“Try assessing the argument before admiring its suit,” the imagined Young replies. “Where is the evidence? What connects it to the claim? Who can follow it? Now ask what the language choices are doing.”</p></details><<button "Replay with a different approach">><<if window.parent === window>><<run setup.choices=[];setup.refresh()>><<else>><<run setup.send("replay")>><</if>><</button>><<button "Return to The Game">><<run setup.send("exit")>><</button>><details><summary>Sources and fiction</summary><p>Core text: Vershawn Ashanti Young, “Should Writers Use They Own English?”, Iowa Journal of Cultural Studies 12 (2010), 110–118. Full article linked on every source card. Read the entire assigned essay; cards are short excerpts and reading guides. Checked October 9, 2026. No generated answer represents Young’s personal view. The AI uses the essay cards and Young’s first-person faculty statement, not an invented corpus of other writings. Episode concepts: objection, concession, qualification, audience, code-meshing, evidence. These are not presented as the weekly vocabulary list.</p></details>
